==English==

===Etymology===
From {{compound|smoke|fall|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|!}}

<!-- PLEASE SEE DISCUSSION PAGE ABOUT SOURCES AND UNCERTAINTIES -->
# The close of the day before [[nightfall]], when [[fog]] comes.
#* '''1935:''' T.S. Eliot, ''Four Quartets'', "Burnt Norton" {{qualifier|possible neologism}}
#*: The moment in the draughty church at '''smokefall'''
# The [[soot]] [[fallout]] from a [[cloud]] of smoke.
#* '''1985:''' T.S. Ledley and S.L. Thompson, ''Potential effect of nuclear war smokefall on sea ice'' {{qualifier|apparent neologism}}
#*: The largest sea ice perturbations are generated by '''smokefall''' in spring.
# An artificial [[waterfall]] of smoke for shows; a smoke fall.

